\title{Mutually Orthogonal Italian Squares:\\ The Sharp Bound $n-1$ and its Prime-Power Realization}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-26}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
An \emph{Italian square} of order $n$ --- synonymous with a \emph{Latin square} --- is an $n \times n$ array over an $n$-symbol alphabet in which every symbol occurs exactly once in each row and exactly once in each column. Two Italian squares are \emph{orthogonal} when their superposition realizes each ordered pair of symbols exactly once. We give a complete, self-contained account of two results on families of pairwise orthogonal Italian squares (MOLS). First, an \textbf{upper bound}: for every order $n \ge 2$, any family of pairwise orthogonal Italian squares has at most $n-1$ members. Second, a \textbf{prime-power realization}: for every prime power $n = p^k \ge 2$, the affine squares $S_a(i,j) = a\cdot i + j$ indexed by the nonzero elements $a$ of the Galois field $\GF(p^k)$ form a family of exactly $n-1$ pairwise orthogonal squares, attaining the bound. Together these give: over a finite field with $n \ge 2$ elements the maximum size of an MOLS family is exactly $n-1$, and it is achieved. We present definitions, main theorems with detailed proof sketches, the affine construction algorithm, worked examples, applications, and a careful discussion of the converse direction --- ``the bound is attained \emph{only} for prime powers'' --- which is equivalent to the open existence problem for finite projective planes and is deliberately left unasserted.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Latin squares are among the oldest studied combinatorial objects, crystallized in Euler's eighteenth-century investigations. A \emph{Latin square} of order $n$ is an $n \times n$ matrix over an alphabet of $n$ symbols such that each symbol appears exactly once in each row and exactly once in each column. We adopt the name \textbf{Italian square} as a synonym throughout; the mathematics is identical.

Two Latin squares are \emph{orthogonal} (a Graeco-Latin pair) if superimposing them produces each of the $n^2$ ordered symbol-pairs exactly once. A family in which every two members are orthogonal is a set of \textbf{mutually orthogonal Latin squares (MOLS)}. The fundamental quantity is
\[
N(n) := \text{the maximum size of a family of pairwise orthogonal Latin squares of order } n.
\]
This paper establishes two cornerstones of MOLS theory:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{(Upper bound)} $N(n) \le n-1$ for all $n \ge 2$;
  \item \textbf{(Prime-power tightness)} $N(n) = n-1$ whenever $n$ is a prime power.
\end{itemize}
The combination is classical (Bose; Van Lint \& Wilson, \emph{A Course in Combinatorics}, Ch.~22; Brualdi \& Dahl 2018); our contribution is a clean, faithful, machine-checked development together with this expository synthesis. We also delimit precisely what is \emph{not} proven: the converse implication --- that the bound is attained only at prime powers --- is equivalent to the existence of finite projective planes and remains open.

\section{Definitions}

We work over an arbitrary symbol type $\alpha$; the order is $n = |\alpha|$ when $\alpha$ is finite.

\begin{definition}[Italian square]
An \emph{Italian square} on a symbol set $\alpha$ is a function $L : \alpha \to \alpha \to \alpha$, written $L(i,j)$ for the entry in row $i$ and column $j$, subject to:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \emph{Row condition} (\texttt{row\_bij}): for every row $i$, the map $j \mapsto L(i,j)$ is a bijection of $\alpha$;
  \item \emph{Column condition} (\texttt{col\_bij}): for every column $j$, the map $i \mapsto L(i,j)$ is a bijection of $\alpha$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

Each condition says ``every symbol occurs exactly once'': once per row, once per column. For finite $\alpha$, a self-map is a bijection iff it is injective iff it is surjective, matching the array intuition.

\begin{definition}[Orthogonality]
Two Italian squares $L,M$ on $\alpha$ are \emph{orthogonal}, written $\Orth(L,M)$, if the superposition map
\[
\Phi_{L,M} : \alpha\times\alpha \to \alpha\times\alpha, \qquad (i,j)\mapsto \big(L(i,j),\,M(i,j)\big)
\]
is a bijection. Equivalently, each ordered pair $(s,t)$ arises from exactly one cell $(i,j)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[MOLS family]
A family $\{L_s\}_{s\in K}$ of Italian squares is \emph{pairwise orthogonal} if $\Orth(L_s,L_t)$ for all $s\ne t$. We write $N(n)$ for the maximum $|K|$ over all such families of order $n$.
\end{definition}

\section{Main results}

\begin{theorem}[Upper bound, \texttt{card\_le\_card\_sub\_one}]\label{thm:ub}
Let $\alpha$ be finite with $|\alpha| = n \ge 2$. For any family $\{L_s\}_{s\in K}$ of pairwise orthogonal Italian squares on $\alpha$, $|K| \le n-1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Affine squares are Italian, \texttt{affine\_row\_bij}, \texttt{affine\_col\_bij}]\label{thm:affine}
Let $F$ be a field and $a \in F$, $a \ne 0$. The array $S_a(i,j) = a\cdot i + j$ satisfies the row and column conditions, so \texttt{affineSquare} defines an Italian square on $F$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Orthogonality of distinct slopes, \texttt{affineSquare\_orthogonal}]\label{thm:orth}
Let $F$ be a field and $a,b\in F$ with $a\ne 0$, $b\ne 0$, $a\ne b$. Then $S_a$ and $S_b$ are orthogonal.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Tightness, \texttt{exists\_mols\_card\_eq\_card\_sub\_one}]\label{thm:tight}
Let $F$ be a finite field with $|F|\ge 2$. There exists a pairwise orthogonal family of Italian squares on $F$ of size exactly $|F|-1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exact maximum, \texttt{maximum\_mols\_eq\_card\_sub\_one}]\label{thm:max}
Let $F$ be a finite field with $|F| = n \ge 2$. The maximum size of a pairwise orthogonal family of Italian squares on $F$ is exactly $n-1$, and it is attained.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Prime-power realization, \texttt{exists\_mols\_prime\_power}]\label{thm:pp}
Let $p$ be prime, $k\ge 1$, $n = p^k \ge 2$. Then there exist $n-1$ pairwise orthogonal Italian squares of order $n$, realized on $\GF(p^k)$.
\end{theorem}

\section{The upper bound \texorpdfstring{$N(n)\le n-1$}{N(n) <= n-1}}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch of Theorem~\ref{thm:ub}]
\textbf{Step 1 (Relabeling invariance).} Orthogonality is invariant under independently permuting the alphabet within each square: replacing $L$ by $\sigma\circ L$ and $M$ by $\tau\circ M$ post-composes $\Phi_{L,M}$ with the bijection $(\sigma,\tau)$, preserving bijectivity, and each relabeled square remains Italian. Hence we may \emph{standardize} each square so its first row is the identity listing, without affecting pairwise orthogonality.

\textbf{Step 2 (Witness cell).} Since $n\ge 2$, pick distinct rows $x_0$ (standardized) and $x_1$ and a column $c$. For each $L_s$ record the symbol $L_s(x_1,c)$.

\textbf{Step 3 (Distinct symbols).} If two standardized squares $L_s,L_t$ ($s\ne t$) place the same $w$ at $(x_1,c)$, then since both have identity first row there is a column $d$ with $L_s(x_0,d)=L_t(x_0,d)=w$. Hence the pair $(w,w)$ occurs in $\Phi_{L_s,L_t}$ at both $(x_1,c)$ and $(x_0,d)$ --- two distinct cells, contradicting orthogonality. So $s\mapsto L_s(x_1,c)$ is injective.

\textbf{Step 4 (Count).} Injectivity gives $|K|\le n$; excluding the single diagonal symbol forced by the first row leaves $\le n-1$ admissible symbols, so $|K|\le n-1$.
\end{proof}

The formal proof avoids materializing standardized squares: for each $L_t$ it uses the bijective first row $\mathrm{row}_0 : j\mapsto L_t(x_0,j)$ to define a slope invariant $a(t) := \mathrm{row}_0^{-1}(L_t(x_1,x_0))$ and shows $t\mapsto a(t)$ is injective into an admissible set of size $n-1$.

\section{The affine construction and tightness}

Let $F$ be a field and, for nonzero $a$, set $S_a(i,j) = a\cdot i + j$.

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{affine\_row\_bij}]
For any $a,i\in F$, the map $j\mapsto a\cdot i + j$ is a bijection of $F$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
It is $j\mapsto j+c$ with $c = a\cdot i$: injective by left-cancellation of addition, surjective since $j = u-c$ solves $j+c=u$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{affine\_col\_bij}]
For $a\ne 0$ and any $j\in F$, the map $i\mapsto a\cdot i + j$ is a bijection of $F$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Injective: $a i_1 + j = a i_2 + j \Rightarrow a i_1 = a i_2 \Rightarrow i_1 = i_2$ by cancelling nonzero $a$. Surjective: for target $u$, take $i = (u-j)/a$, so $a\cdot\frac{u-j}{a} + j = u$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch of Theorem~\ref{thm:orth}]
We show $\Phi:(i,j)\mapsto(ai+j,\,bi+j)$ is a bijection of $F\times F$.

\emph{Injectivity.} If $ai_1+j_1 = ai_2+j_2$ and $bi_1+j_1 = bi_2+j_2$, subtracting eliminates $j$: $(a-b)i_1 = (a-b)i_2$; since $a\ne b$, cancel $a-b\ne0$ to get $i_1=i_2$, then $j_1=j_2$.

\emph{Surjectivity.} For target $(u,v)$ solve $ai+j=u$, $bi+j=v$. Subtracting: $(a-b)i = u-v$, so $i = \frac{u-v}{a-b}$ and $j = u-ai$.

The argument is the invertibility of $\left(\begin{smallmatrix} a&1\\ b&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)$, whose determinant is $a-b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorems~\ref{thm:tight}--\ref{thm:pp}]
Theorem~\ref{thm:tight}: the family $\{S_a : a\ne 0\}$ is pairwise orthogonal by Theorem~\ref{thm:orth}, and is indexed by the complement of $\{0\}$, of size $|F|-1$. Theorem~\ref{thm:max}: combine Theorem~\ref{thm:tight} (lower) with Theorem~\ref{thm:ub} (upper). Theorem~\ref{thm:pp}: $\GF(p^k)$ has exactly $n=p^k$ elements (valid since $k\ne0$); apply Theorem~\ref{thm:tight}.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Affine MOLS generator.} Given a field of order $n$, output the $n-1$ squares $S_a(i,j)=a\cdot i+j$ for $a\ne0$.
\begin{verbatim}
for each a in F with a != 0:
    S_a := new n x n array
    for i in F: for j in F: S_a[i][j] := a*i + j
    append S_a
\end{verbatim}
Cost: $O(n^2)$ per square, $O(n^3)$ for the family.

\paragraph{Orthogonality verifier.} Collect the $n^2$ pairs $(L(i,j),M(i,j))$ and check distinctness; $O(n^2)$ time.

\section{Worked examples}

\textbf{Order 3} ($\mathbb{Z}/3\mathbb{Z}$): slopes $1,2$ give the maximum $2$ squares
\[
S_1=\begin{smallmatrix}0&1&2\\1&2&0\\2&0&1\end{smallmatrix},\qquad
S_2=\begin{smallmatrix}0&1&2\\2&0&1\\1&2&0\end{smallmatrix},
\]
whose superposition lists all nine pairs once.

\textbf{Order 4} ($\GF(4)$): $4=2^2$ is a prime power but not prime; one uses $\GF(4)=\{0,1,\omega,\omega^2\}$ with $\omega^2=\omega+1$, \emph{not} $\mathbb{Z}/4\mathbb{Z}$. The three nonzero slopes give $3$ MOLS, the maximum.

\textbf{Order 5} ($\mathbb{Z}/5\mathbb{Z}$): slopes $1,2,3,4$ give the full ceiling $4$.

\textbf{Order 6} (exceptional): $6$ is not a prime power; Tarry (1900) proved $N(6)=1<5$. This lives on the open converse side.

\section{Applications}

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Experimental design:} Graeco-Latin and hyper-Graeco-Latin designs, foundational to Fisher's analysis of variance.
  \item \textbf{Coding theory:} a complete set of $n-1$ MOLS is equivalent to an MDS code / orthogonal array of strength 2, index 1; the bound is the Singleton bound in disguise.
  \item \textbf{Finite geometry:} a complete MOLS family of order $n$ is equivalent to an affine (hence projective) plane of order $n$; slopes are parallel classes.
  \item \textbf{Cryptography:} the affine maps $x\mapsto ax+b$ form a sharply 2-transitive group, underlying authentication codes and universal hashing.
  \item \textbf{Scheduling:} round-robin tournaments, timetabling, frequency assignment.
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion: proven vs.\ open}

We rigorously establish both halves of the prime-power statement: the universal upper bound $N(n)\le n-1$ and the prime-power tightness $N(n)=n-1$. We deliberately do \emph{not} assert the converse ``attained only for prime powers,'' equivalent to: a finite projective plane of order $n$ exists iff $n$ is a prime power --- whose reverse direction is a celebrated open problem. Boundary facts framing the gap: Bruck--Ryser--Chowla (excludes $n=6,14,21,22,\dots$); Tarry's $N(6)=1$; nonexistence of a plane of order 10 by computer search; and the Bose--Shrikhande--Parker refutation of Euler's broader conjecture.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Plane equivalence:} complete $(n-1)$-MOLS exist iff a projective plane of order $n$ exists.
  \item \textbf{Bruck--Ryser--Chowla obstruction:} a falsifiable necessary condition from elementary number theory.
  \item \textbf{MacNeish multiplicative bound:} $N(mn)\ge\min(N(m),N(n))$ via the Kronecker product of squares.
  \item \textbf{Order-6 nonexistence:} a decidable finite search verifying $N(6)=1$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

For prime powers the answer is exact and beautiful: $N(n)=n-1$, attained by the affine squares $S_a(i,j)=ai+j$. The upper bound flows from a one-cell pigeonhole argument; the matching construction from the single fact that nonzero field elements are invertible. Whether non-prime-power orders can ever reach the ceiling remains a deep open problem, and our development proves exactly what can be proven.

